@inject RulebookReader Book

@* What the book prints about this Power, for somebody who is signed in.

   **It renders nothing at all when there is nothing to show**, and that is the whole of its
   error handling. Not signed in, no account, the server not deployed, or a Power the book files
   under another heading — Super Senses' sixteen options share one printed entry between them —
   are all the same to a player, and a row of apologies under a fifth of the Powers would be
   worse than silence. `RulebookReader` answers null for every one of them.

   **A disclosure rather than a Tooltip.** A tooltip is sized and positioned for a sentence; a
   Power's entry is several paragraphs, and it is something to read rather than to glance at.

   The mechanics above it come from `data/rules`, which is verified entry by entry and is what
   the engine costs against. This is the text beside them, never instead of them.

   **`BookText` owns how a passage is set**, so this page and the rules reference cannot present
   the same text two ways. This one only decides *which* passage and where it is printed. *@

@if (_entry is { } entry)
{
    <div class="book">
        @* aria-expanded is written out as a string. Blazor drops a bool attribute when it is
           false and renders `aria-expanded=""` when it is true, and empty is invalid ARIA that
           assistive technology reads as *not* expanded — so the natural spelling announces the
           opposite of the state in both directions. *@
        <button type="button" class="book-toggle"
                aria-expanded="@(_open ? "true" : "false")"
                aria-controls="@(_open ? BodyId : null)"
                @onclick="() => _open = !_open">
            @(_open ? "Hide" : "Show") what the book says
        </button>

        @* Rendered only when open, so `aria-controls` above is only set then: naming an element
           that is not in the document leaves a dangling IDREF, and `aria-expanded` is what
           carries the state. Same rule as the budget breakdown. *@
        @if (_open)
        {
            <div id="@BodyId">
                <BookText Text="@entry.Text"
                          Citation="@($"{entry.SourceRef}, printed p.{entry.PrintedPage}")" />
            </div>
        }
    </div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The Power to show the printed entry for, by the name the rules data gives it.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string PowerName { get; set; } = "";

    private PowerEntry? _entry;
    private bool _open;

    /// <summary>
    /// Derived from the Power's name rather than generated, so two renders of one Power produce
    /// identical markup. A generated id differs per render, which breaks the replay guard
    /// requiring two renders of one character to be the same — the trap `Tooltip` already hit.
    /// </summary>
    private string BodyId => "book-" + PowerName.ToLowerInvariant().Replace(' ', '-');

    protected override async Task OnParametersSetAsync()
    {
        // Cleared first: the editor is reused for the next Power opened, and holding the last
        // one's entry would print somebody else's rules under this Power's name.
        _entry = null;
        _open = false;

        _entry = await Book.ForPowerAsync(PowerName);
    }
}
